From: FingerBrowser
Subject: [PATCH 0016] hide CJK fonts and remap generics for a non-CJK persona

A non-CJK persona (en-US, …) running on a Chinese/Japanese/Korean Windows
leaks its true region through fonts: measurement-based enumeration finds the
installed CJK fonts (Microsoft YaHei, SimSun, …), and the generic families
themselves resolve to CJK fonts (on a Chinese Windows sans-serif renders as
YaHei), so even generic Latin text exposes the host locale.

Two coordinated parts, gated by the fp-block-cjk-fonts switch:

1. Explicit-family enumeration: FontCache::GetFontData and
   IsPlatformFamilyMatchAvailable report blocked CJK families as unavailable.
   This is the path canvas measureText / CSS font-family use. It is NOT on the
   shared GetFontPlatformData/CreateFontPlatformData path, so the DirectWrite
   system glyph fallback (PlatformFallbackFontForCharacter) still renders CJK
   text -- no tofu.

2. Generic remap: OverrideWebkitPrefs sets the common/Latin ("Zyyy"/"Latn")
   entry of each generic font map to a US-Windows font (sans-serif->Arial,
   serif->Times New Roman, monospace->Consolas, …). Without this, blocking the
   CJK fonts that the generics default to collapses them onto a wrong
   last-resort (monospace turns proportional). With it, the generics render
   like a US machine and stop leaking the region.

Depends on: 0001-0015. Verified on a rebuilt kernel: YaHei/Malgun drop out of
enumeration, sans-serif renders as Arial (not YaHei), monospace stays a real
monospace, Chinese text still renders, and none of it triggers without the
switch.

diff --git a/chrome/browser/chrome_content_browser_client.cc b/chrome/browser/chrome_content_browser_client.cc
index cc91620a6e..18fe5aed4e 100644
--- a/chrome/browser/chrome_content_browser_client.cc
+++ b/chrome/browser/chrome_content_browser_client.cc
@@ -4003,6 +4003,28 @@ void ChromeContentBrowserClient::OverrideWebkitPrefs(
                                        &web_prefs->math_font_family_map);
   }
 
+  // FingerBrowser: on a CJK Windows the generic font families (sans-serif,
+  // serif, monospace) default to CJK fonts -- their common-script ("Zyyy")
+  // entry is e.g. Microsoft YaHei. That both leaks the host region (sans-serif
+  // Latin text renders as YaHei) and, once the CJK-font block hides those
+  // families, collapses the generics onto a wrong last-resort (monospace turns
+  // proportional). Remap the common/Latin generic entries to US-Windows fonts
+  // so the generics render like a US machine. CJK text still renders via glyph
+  // fallback; only the explicit-family enumeration path is blocked elsewhere.
+  if (base::CommandLine::ForCurrentProcess()->HasSwitch("fp-block-cjk-fonts")) {
+    const auto set_generic =
+        [](blink::web_pref::ScriptFontFamilyMap* map, const char16_t* font) {
+          (*map)["Zyyy"] = font;  // USCRIPT_COMMON: the default/Latin generic
+          (*map)["Latn"] = font;
+        };
+    set_generic(&web_prefs->standard_font_family_map, u"Times New Roman");
+    set_generic(&web_prefs->serif_font_family_map, u"Times New Roman");
+    set_generic(&web_prefs->sans_serif_font_family_map, u"Arial");
+    set_generic(&web_prefs->fixed_font_family_map, u"Consolas");
+    set_generic(&web_prefs->cursive_font_family_map, u"Comic Sans MS");
+    set_generic(&web_prefs->fantasy_font_family_map, u"Impact");
+  }
+
   web_prefs->default_font_size =
       prefs->GetInteger(prefs::kWebKitDefaultFontSize);
   web_prefs->default_fixed_font_size =
